  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on the minimum probability—defined as the smallest chance of any outcome (i.e., the chance of winning), and prefers the lottery with the higher such value. The individual, in contrast, refers to 'lower losses' and seems to focus on the probability or experience of losing, preferring the option with a slightly lower chance of getting nothing (99.8% vs 99.9%). While both the reason and the individual consider probabilities, the decision reason focuses on the positive outcome's probability (minimum probability = chance of winning), whereas the individual focuses on the negative side (chance of losing). The information considered (losses vs. minimum probability of any outcome) and the justification (favoring lower losses) do not align with the decision reason’s rule. Therefore, it is unlikely the individual used this specific decision reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 20
